[Event "Italian Game: Giuoco Piano"]
[RepertoireName "Italian Game (White)"]
[Repertoire "white"]
[ChapterName "Giuoco Piano with 4.c3"]
[Result "*"]

{The slow Italian: build with c3 and d3, castle, then expand with a4 and h3. Few forcing lines, lots of plans.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 {The bishop eyes f7, the softest square in Black's camp. [%csl Rf7]} Bc5
4. c3 {Preparing d4 one day, and giving the bishop a retreat square on b3 or c2.} Nf6
5. d3 {The quiet move. We keep e4 protected and avoid early simplifications.} d6
(5... a6 6. O-O d6 7. a4 {Space on the queenside so the Bc4 can't be harassed by ...b5. [%cal Ga2a4]})
(5... O-O 6. O-O d5 {Black strikes in the centre straight away.} 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. a4 {Same idea: gain space and keep the bishop's diagonal.})
6. O-O a6 7. a4 O-O 8. h3 {Stops ...Bg4 and its pin on the f3-knight. [%cal Gh2h3,Rc8g4]} Ba7 9. Re1 {The rook backs up e4 and prepares Nbd2–f1–g3, the classic Italian manoeuvre.} *

[Event "Italian Game: Two Knights"]
[ChapterName "Two Knights with 4.d3"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 {We sidestep the sharp 4.Ng5 lines and keep a solid centre.} Be7
(4... Bc5 5. c3 {Transposes to the Giuoco Piano chapter. The trainer recognises the position, so you are not quizzed twice.})
(4... d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 6. O-O {Castle first; the d5-knight is a target for Re1 and Nc3 later.})
(4... h6 5. c3 d6 6. O-O)
5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {The same structure as the Giuoco Piano: a4, h3 and Nbd2 come next.} *

[Event "Italian Game: Hungarian Defence"]
[ChapterName "Hungarian Defence 3...Be7"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Be7 {Passive. Black wants to avoid theory and play ...d6 and ...Nf6.} 4. d4 {When Black is passive, take the centre.} d6
(4... exd4 5. Nxd4 Nf6 6. Nc3 O-O 7. O-O d6 8. h3 {A pleasant Scotch-like position with more space for White.})
5. d5 {Gain space with tempo on the knight.} Nb8 6. Bd3 {Clearing the c-pawn's path so c4 can follow.} Nf6 7. c4 *
